 formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "682a228b-b79d-5fcb-b15e-e67fdbaa8be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537024} {:answer/id #uuid "6af500dd-3fdb-56d1-8617-670cec01eb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537028} {:answer/id #uuid "7301b34c-817c-56a3-8683-9aaaf6122853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537032} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cad40a-69b5-528c-86d1-d835cef665eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537069} {:answer/id #uuid "e796db0a-5eac-585e-902e-dc1ce488d7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{10}^{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7301b34c-817c-56a3-8683-9aaaf6122853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537032} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c84f22-679b-57d9-9b4d-6d3035a36e20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de103878-75c8-5f73-9ce2-41a2334b4eac" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 10\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d5ba5f13ed464fa4e2b53fb2cd4fbdab92b3de371d3f7b535aeac8d0adb6d5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le 10\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fafcac-b57c-5a49-aadc-f7ea513877d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536990} {:answer/id #uuid "208a838f-c0e2-54fe-b2e6-dc7e12747ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536993} {:answer/id #uuid "7084a39e-ec6e-5c74-b493-fb831dae5598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537031} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f22e85-6995-51aa-910a-ab0f4ae5632a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537054} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd1b677-e296-592e-b708-0635f034aaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "208a838f-c0e2-54fe-b2e6-dc7e12747ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536993} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac38c3ec-fa0a-5519-a3eb-cc2f31de45a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eea1a108-64c8-59f3-971b-a2f53cbd0dc5" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555026} {:answer/id #uuid "24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555073} {:answer/id #uuid "26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555081} {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} {:answer/id #uuid "acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" :question/math-academy-id "q-161628" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555089} {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} {:answer/id #uuid "c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555462} {:answer/id #uuid "f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555596} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-161631" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137dcd13-d6dd-5100-aa1a-4ef83376e70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564970} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} {:answer/id #uuid "8156f8ac-e493-504e-aade-245eb369fa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565139} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6bc81a-88bf-5b77-b8c7-6ed75d98d631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565174} {:answer/id #uuid "dd82ed50-ec04-508c-a578-c8dd2bbd72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d4b2ef-ed57-5651-8502-b9160bd5e70c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66285a95-784c-5363-bee3-101afc12cafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161635" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{3}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c0fc71-469e-590c-ba8b-284e07e9d56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600512} {:answer/id #uuid "49003dc7-d1f6-5706-9ec6-4e81c0fa6b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600563} {:answer/id #uuid "4ada50cc-a17f-524a-b317-1e1efb616136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600567} {:answer/id #uuid "c950dfc3-14f5-582e-8ccb-600b02b54ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600660} {:answer/id #uuid "e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600684} :answer-field/id #uuid "69082d16-ac02-5957-841e-63c1d5f5e644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce2ff483-7446-5ef4-9532-e3d923aa91d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161634" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0,\\,x\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/27/27a82956e22b5b2e2de2c02d9d93fb7b7ce57a2b581b88458964eda465e8bebd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1={r}^{2}+1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\left(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}\\right))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539392 :question-generator/id #uuid "88a49b66-1e9c-5b35-8e4c-127a3b9a4c84" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186198361.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "994baf38-6869-57c8-804b-1e89962812d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}uvdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197559} {:answer/id #uuid "d214aef4-d04e-532d-9308-a674c66f4c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(v^{2} - u^{2})dvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197560} {:answer/id #uuid "ead9db94-80ba-523a-bbee-88ad906b59cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}uvdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197561} {:answer/id #uuid "384bbcf9-30ea-557e-836f-3cd316ae1965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}uvdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197562} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef5fead-0f57-5a03-9698-dea1aaf85a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(u^{2} - v^{2})dvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197563}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3475359c-b0e8-57d9-b322-9db73816c054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197558}] :question/id #uuid "bbd81848-9466-52f1-b1b2-16ba771211ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-38129" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x + y \\le 3, 0 \\le y - x \\le 2}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y$\nand\n$v = y - x$,\nthe integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy\n$$\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493498} {:answer/id #uuid "c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493712} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493730} {:answer/id #uuid "f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ad75dc-fe53-5e66-9950-00b2a3fe62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1c41fe9-56fa-5a4c-8bab-8d810d33bac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38371" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e344f475-3668-5c1f-9b88-0fa12f4ef4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373911} {:answer/id #uuid "17a3824c-bebc-5701-8d19-9114502785b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373912} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f6312-74e7-571d-b7fe-be323a268da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373913} {:answer/id #uuid "22d2eda0-0d96-5f44-84d6-338243168c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373914} {:answer/id #uuid "fcec1dbc-cba7-5afe-82ab-ccf5f65b004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcec1dbc-cba7-5afe-82ab-ccf5f65b004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373915} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8dfde2a-78d4-5f4f-9dd7-f750b4f43d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c98b0d83-24f7-5020-a2b4-fd331038c974" :question/math-academy-id "q-160897" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,1\\le xy\\le 5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}v|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}v[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}v[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot (25-1) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e43e2c3-5048-5e2e-a94a-bcb90d586202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-{u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375105} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} {:answer/id #uuid "f152898a-1a3f-57ba-8463-c69b4da0c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-{v}^{2}+3{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375107} {:answer/id #uuid "3b55205c-68ca-554b-93eb-3fc21cfe0e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-2{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375108} {:answer/id #uuid "11837235-5be9-5816-bdf3-58b69896ee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-5{v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} :answer-field/id #uuid "227ed5b3-0cfb-51b9-820b-b40696387d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db1fee9d-f261-54f6-9563-ad9a8f609d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38369" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+y\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+y$ and $v=y,$ the integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{\\left(\\frac{u-v}{2}\\right)}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0881d141-176a-5207-829e-e7fcf9748ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{4}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375497} {:answer/id #uuid "72802f78-8fa2-5be5-8cb9-94054dadb960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} {:answer/id #uuid "9d50078b-ccc0-5312-be32-aa00de941511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375499} {:answer/id #uuid "ce22a068-82fa-5eda-a3a4-b436e880bc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375500} {:answer/id #uuid "f22feb1c-862a-595f-aaba-980169e06dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72802f78-8fa2-5be5-8cb9-94054dadb960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b052ab4-b874-5259-8af3-5e39e362471e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff0203f2-b0b4-5368-8eaf-f5cdf929fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-82808" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=y-x$ and $v=x+y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8\\left(\\frac{v-u}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v+u}{2}\\right)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "661f11a2-58e2-5323-aed0-4c84d298b7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3u-2v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375504} {:answer/id #uuid "ac200449-dc33-5fa1-b080-2757eb77e01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375505} {:answer/id #uuid "32bc657c-6f00-59b6-8ae4-b48679d4067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375506} {:answer/id #uuid "abd6ec5f-e42a-51e9-a175-fd591e2adebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{u+v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375507} {:answer/id #uuid "624cfbb7-2e59-5fce-9fc2-e4f5ba306b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3u-2v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac200449-dc33-5fa1-b080-2757eb77e01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375505} :answer-field/id #uuid "50dace88-55f0-5aaf-a1fc-7d3cc1e32c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d437ff63-9f7b-5463-a5bf-dc3f2e150b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-82827" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+5y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+5y$ and $v=x+3y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=3u-5v,\\,y=2v-u.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{3u-5v+2v-u}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b519e50-b326-56a3-8360-01cf01bef0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375511} {:answer/id #uuid "8717345d-af4e-5f8b-876a-46ab0b5392c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375512} {:answer/id #uuid "de05559f-528d-50d8-9fa6-bf9cc362a558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375513} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4bc111-f102-5feb-91f1-f0edd3cc5425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecb057f-1869-565e-8e7e-c9b2d50df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e4bc111-f102-5feb-91f1-f0edd3cc5425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} :answer-field/id #uuid "da395623-3256-578e-8977-74807708542d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab9f14a-bb15-51f5-bbda-2778b7330c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83647" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4c86f2-5cfb-5693-a142-edc6be59008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480767} {:answer/id #uuid "344eed9f-15e2-5d9d-a328-322b0adeb917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480839} {:answer/id #uuid "68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480933} {:answer/id #uuid "79ad7697-010f-568d-851d-4911a16f499a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480962} {:answer/id #uuid "95eca681-53e5-51f4-8569-95f9733c2dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" :question/math-academy-id "q-83656" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u={e}^{x}$ and $v={e}^{y},$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538437b0-2f11-5cf6-a513-3b5f95549b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480889} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec46383-2d2f-5cde-b1c3-b222f3cbe2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480947} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480968} {:answer/id #uuid "8271e591-b018-5489-b0eb-360dc3d744e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(2u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480988} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd4b6c-e4fe-5b75-be7e-3479440b02e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480968} :answer-field/id #uuid "912cb459-ddba-5110-ba83-e5c7cb9c0fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-82798" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480830} {:answer/id #uuid "50d5f25b-3bff-52d9-b4d2-40d73df06c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480888} {:answer/id #uuid "632142b3-3dde-53ef-9fc3-71eb83cdcd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480921} {:answer/id #uuid "844f29d7-a7a5-5823-a2f4-5f8ba38566ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480993} {:answer/id #uuid "b71f8dcb-5c9d-54b5-b09b-deeeea423e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480830} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab7f9c1-3d45-58a6-bbb6-a63febcef757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-82818" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480922} {:answer/id #uuid "8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480987} {:answer/id #uuid "91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481012} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481154} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" :question/math-academy-id "q-160850" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e55ce08-51f3-5f7d-9882-1ad73c59bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480765} {:answer/id #uuid "8273dafa-bda1-5a66-9cff-9b7575876a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480989} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481100} {:answer/id #uuid "e59d2e21-5875-56c6-8566-91c968ba9e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481175} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a5b9c-1081-52b7-b89e-e7f3524f0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481100} :answer-field/id #uuid "7505a4fd-3c5e-5a38-a1f7-3891a3c2965c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82911" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x-y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493410} {:answer/id #uuid "17dcff5e-6574-5e2e-be4c-22c67c1a31d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493432} {:answer/id #uuid "702b0bc2-ac10-5437-9f18-9fa877532a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493554} {:answer/id #uuid "857734df-e10d-5568-a049-4e87b8b5c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493601} {:answer/id #uuid "e162164d-9035-55fa-9f1c-b47ea0bae241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}-e)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493410} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ea503e-c96f-567a-8f89-cf26cc3e1193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160899" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\le y\\le 2x,\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493428} {:answer/id #uuid "37392f66-38d1-5cf1-a83a-b61ffbecdc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493480} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9d745e-3b7a-5566-acd4-df51a55a0434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493492} {:answer/id #uuid "757701d0-e96c-58b7-9b6d-52ab1067ccc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493565} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b8b11d-0fcd-5a7a-8d95-67c9db317553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493428} :answer-field/id #uuid "b260735c-8ce2-5820-82bd-e9db5e7533a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-82985" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+3y$ and $v=x+2y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+2y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4498327b-6560-51a2-b3e5-e74c8480729b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539363} {:answer/id #uuid "4a95c0e4-a164-5d09-9222-9c149c45ab7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539365} {:answer/id #uuid "6e41dde2-dfa2-5092-b54e-8a8248dcf4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{123 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539374} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c5a825-f639-5ea1-9df7-8def67861623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539382} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb39b70-4ea5-52bd-a660-df7df68c411f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0c5a825-f639-5ea1-9df7-8def67861623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539382} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e20894-f26f-5e9b-8de6-fcc616806ae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ddd94b2-cc71-504e-a7a5-1df422f9b408" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,4\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{4}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{122\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49693d90-a88f-5cc1-8d9c-4cdbfd34c316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "203 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539364} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce3c7dc-caf4-59c6-bc90-66911b31b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 52 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539370} {:answer/id #uuid "66c4bff9-f18e-5a0a-ae8c-565ea3df5f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539372} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1f244f-0b28-5817-86a7-3ca216955106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539378} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dbc465-6dd3-5c31-801d-8e21abfc3c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b1f244f-0b28-5817-86a7-3ca216955106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539378} :answer-field/id #uuid "189e4d5c-7d67-5000-8ba2-67a9c86a5254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5fc233a3-016d-5c4e-a242-9f02a02751f2" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 16{x}^{2},\\,2\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (125-8) \\\\  & =26\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088d3881-6f26-5c1e-8b70-605ffcbe6a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539357} {:answer/id #uuid "13d60485-67ff-563a-a2f9-a27075387c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{112 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539358} {:answer/id #uuid "4e49411c-8515-54df-bf3e-1b739c795ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{112 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218